Q: XLSX- how to get rid of the default namespace prefix x:? A: <p>Very old post... but same problem in 2026 ! The &quot;x:&quot; namespace is still added and can cause problem on some other software that import XLSX.</p>
<p>Here's our hack :</p>
<p>When creating the speadsheet, just before disposing, we call 2 customs functions :</p>
<pre><code>SpreadsheetDocument spreadsheetDoc = DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging.SpreadsheetDocument.Open(this._spreadsheetDocumentStream, true)

// your code that generate the XLSX

// Fixing problem :
FixAllWorksheetDimensions(spreadsheetDoc); //only needed if software report wrong number of lines
RemovePrefixesEverywhere(spreadsheetDoc); //remove bad namespace

spreadsheetDoc.Dispose();
</code></pre>
<p>And here's the customs functions :</p>
<pre><code>private void RemovePrefixesEverywhere(SpreadsheetDocument doc)
{
    foreach (var part in doc.GetAllParts())
    {
        try
        {
            string xml;
            using (var reader = new StreamReader(part.GetStream(FileMode.Open, FileAccess.Read)))
            {
                xml = reader.ReadToEnd();
            }
            if (xml.StartsWith(&quot;&lt;?xml&quot;))
            {
                // remove all &quot;x:&quot; prefixes and xmlns:x declaration
                xml = xml.Replace(&quot;&lt;x:&quot;, &quot;&lt;&quot;).Replace(&quot;&lt;/x:&quot;, &quot;&lt;/&quot;).Replace(&quot;xmlns:x=&quot;, &quot;xmlns=&quot;);
                using (var writer = new StreamWriter(part.GetStream(FileMode.Create, FileAccess.Write)))
                {
                    writer.Write(xml);
                }
            }

        } catch { /* ignore parts that are not XML (e.g. images) */ }
    }
}
private void FixAllWorksheetDimensions(SpreadsheetDocument doc)
{
    foreach (var wsPart in doc.WorkbookPart.WorksheetParts)
    {
        var sheetData = wsPart.Worksheet.GetFirstChild&lt;SheetData&gt;();
        if (sheetData == null || !sheetData.Elements&lt;Row&gt;().Any())
            continue;

        uint minRow = sheetData.Elements&lt;Row&gt;().Min(r =&gt; r.RowIndex.Value);
        uint maxRow = sheetData.Elements&lt;Row&gt;().Max(r =&gt; r.RowIndex.Value);

        // max length = number of columns of the longest row
        int maxColIndex = sheetData.Elements&lt;Row&gt;()
                                   .Max(r =&gt; r.Elements&lt;Cell&gt;().Count());
        string maxCol = GetExcelColumnReference(maxColIndex);

        string dimensionRef = $&quot;A{minRow}:{maxCol}{maxRow}&quot;;
        var dim = wsPart.Worksheet.GetFirstChild&lt;SheetDimension&gt;();
        if (dim == null)
        {
            dim = new SheetDimension();
            wsPart.Worksheet.InsertAt(dim, 0);
        }

        dim.Reference = new DocumentFormat.OpenXml.StringValue(dimensionRef);
        wsPart.Worksheet.Save();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Enjoy it :-)</p>

Q: Windows errors in MAUI: how to understand the real error? A: <p>I dont see exception handlers in <code>App.xaml.cs</code>.  Please have them incorporated. First‑Chance exceptions fire as soon as they are thrown. Even if they are caught later, it often reveals the true cause</p>
<pre><code>using System;
using System.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.UI.Xaml;

public partial class App : Application
{
    public App()
    {
        this.UnhandledException += OnWinUiUnhandledException;

        AppDomain.CurrentDomain.UnhandledException += (_, e) =&gt;
        {
            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[AppDomain.Unhandled] {e.ExceptionObject}&quot;);
        };

        AppDomain.CurrentDomain.FirstChanceException += (_, e) =&gt;
        {
            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[FirstChance] {e.Exception}&quot;);
        };
    }

    private void OnWinUiUnhandledException(object sender, Microsoft.UI.Xaml.UnhandledExceptionEventArgs e)
    {
        Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[WinUI.Unhandled] {e.Exception} | Message: {e.Message}&quot;);
        Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[StackTrace] {e.Exception?.StackTrace}&quot;);
        e.Handled = false; // allow debugger to break; set true only if you handle it
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>XAML parse errors are common on Windows (implicit style keys, default resources differences). Add diagnostics to your <code>App.xaml.cs</code> and pages <code>Microsoft.UI.Xaml.DebugSettings.IsBindingTracingEnabled = true;</code>  Also add logging to in your views as well as on VM Constructors and also in ViewModels, log DI resolution and platform services:</p>
<pre><code>public MyPage()
{
    InitializeComponent();
    System.Diagnostics.Debug.WriteLine(&quot;[MyPage] ctor&quot;);
}

protected override void OnNavigatedTo(NavigationEventArgs e)
{
    System.Diagnostics.Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[MyPage] NavigatedTo: {e.Parameter}&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>public MyViewModel(IServiceA a, IServiceB b)
{
    Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[VM] Resolved A={a!=null}, B={b!=null}&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Try all these and see if you can isolate the issues</p>
<p>Updated answer after further comments:</p>
<p>These should be registered as early as possible (e.g., in <code>App()</code> ctor or <code>MauiProgram.CreateMauiApp()</code>). These will log exceptions on Android, iOS, Windows, and Mac Catalyst.</p>
<pre><code>using System;
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public partial class App : Application
{
    public App()
    {

        AppDomain.CurrentDomain.FirstChanceException += (_, e) =&gt;
            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[FirstChance] {e.Exception}&quot;);

        AppDomain.CurrentDomain.UnhandledException += (_, e) =&gt;
            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[AppDomain.Unhandled] {e.ExceptionObject}&quot;);

        TaskScheduler.UnobservedTaskException += (_, e) =&gt;
        {
            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[TaskScheduler.Unobserved] {e.Exception}&quot;);
            e.SetObserved();
        };
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>For native Objective‑C exceptions and crashes (binding issues, UIKit/AppKit problems), you can add Apple‑side handlers. In MAUI, iOS and Mac Catalyst share the same <code>AppDelegate</code> (derived from <code>MauiUIApplicationDelegate</code>), so you can initialize them there with conditional compilation. Register in AppDelegate</p>
<pre><code>#if MACCATALYST
using Foundation;
using ObjCRuntime;
#endif
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace YourNamespace;

public class AppDelegate : MauiUIApplicationDelegate
{
    protected override MauiApp CreateMauiApp() =&gt; MauiProgram.CreateMauiApp();

    public override bool FinishedLaunching(UIKit.UIApplication app, Foundation.NSDictionary options)
    {
        RegisterManagedExceptionHandlers();
        RegisterAppleNativeExceptionHandlers();
        return base.FinishedLaunching(app, options);
    }

    private static void RegisterManagedExceptionHandlers()
    {
        AppDomain.CurrentDomain.FirstChanceException += (_, e) =&gt;
            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[FirstChance] {e.Exception}&quot;);

        AppDomain.CurrentDomain.UnhandledException += (_, e) =&gt;
            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[AppDomain.Unhandled] {e.ExceptionObject}&quot;);

        TaskScheduler.UnobservedTaskException += (_, e) =&gt;
        {
            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[TaskScheduler.Unobserved] {e.Exception}&quot;);
            e.SetObserved();
        };
    }

    private static void RegisterAppleNativeExceptionHandlers()
    {
#if MACCATALYST

        NSSetUncaughtExceptionHandler((exception) =&gt;
        {
            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[ObjC.Uncaught] {exception?.Description}&quot;);
            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;[ObjC.Uncaught.Stack] {exception?.CallStackSymbols}&quot;);
        });

 // Optionally subscribe to NSException notifications (rarely needed):
        //NSNotificationCenter.DefaultCenter.AddObserver(Foundation.NSException.ThrowExceptionNotification, (note) =&gt; { ... });

 // You can also consider signal handlers (SIGABRT, SIGSEGV) via libc if needed.

#endif
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>But be seriously cautious while handling crash signals as it is advanced and can interfere with diagnostics.</p>

Q: FPS character rigidbody rotation axis issue A: <p>The unwanted rotation happens because friction applies torque to your collider when it scrapes against walls or the floor, causing it to roll.</p>
<p>You don't need to counter this with code. Instead, you should rely on the Rigidbody settings to handle the physics state, while handling inputs in <code>Update</code> to keep things smooth.</p>
<p>Here is how I fixed the script and the setup:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Unwanted Rotation:</strong> In the Rigidbody component, expand <strong>Constraints</strong> and check <strong>Freeze Rotation X, Y, Z</strong>. This stops the physics engine from tumbling your player, but still allows you to rotate it via script.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>The &quot;FPS Drop&quot;:</strong> That jitter isn't a performance drop; it's a desync between the physics tick (50Hz) and your monitor's refresh rate. Setting <strong>Interpolate</strong> to &quot;Interpolate&quot; on the Rigidbody fixes this immediately.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Input Lag:</strong> I moved your input reading to <code>Update()</code>. Reading inputs (especially <code>WasPressed</code>) inside <code>FixedUpdate()</code> causes missed clicks because physics updates don't run every frame.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is the cleaned-up controller:</p>
<pre><code>using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.InputSystem;

public class PlayerControl : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] Camera p_camera;
    [SerializeField] InputActionAsset p_inputMap;

    private InputAction m_lookAction;
    private InputAction m_moveAction;
    private InputAction m_jumpAction;

    private Rigidbody rb;
    private float xRotation;

    // Cache inputs to prevent missed clicks
    private Vector2 _currentMoveInput;
    private bool _jumpInputReceived;

    [Header(&quot;Settings&quot;)]
    [SerializeField] float sensitivity = 10f;
    [SerializeField] float maxSpeed = 8f;
    [SerializeField] float jumpForce = 5f;
    [SerializeField] float groundAccel = 50f;
    [SerializeField] float airAccel = 10f;
    [SerializeField] float deAccel = 10f;

    [SerializeField] bool isGround;

    private void Awake()
    {
        rb = GetComponent&lt;Rigidbody&gt;();

        // Important for smooth camera movement
        rb.interpolation = RigidbodyInterpolation.Interpolate;

        // Prevents the player from tipping over due to physics friction
        rb.constraints = RigidbodyConstraints.FreezeRotation;

        Cursor.lockState = CursorLockMode.Locked;
        Cursor.visible = false;

        m_lookAction = p_inputMap.FindAction(&quot;Player/Look&quot;);
        m_moveAction = p_inputMap.FindAction(&quot;Player/Move&quot;);
        m_jumpAction = p_inputMap.FindAction(&quot;Player/Jump&quot;);

        m_lookAction.Enable();
        m_moveAction.Enable();
        m_jumpAction.Enable();
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        // Always read inputs in Update so you don't miss frames
        _currentMoveInput = m_moveAction.ReadValue&lt;Vector2&gt;();

        if (m_jumpAction.WasPressedThisFrame())
        {
            _jumpInputReceived = true;
        }

        // Camera must be in Update, otherwise it looks laggy on high refresh rate monitors
        HandleLook();
    }

    private void FixedUpdate()
    {
        // Apply physics forces here
        HandleMovement();
        HandleJump();
    }

    private void HandleLook()
    {
        Vector2 lookInput = m_lookAction.ReadValue&lt;Vector2&gt;();

        float mouseX = lookInput.x * sensitivity * Time.deltaTime;
        float mouseY = lookInput.y * sensitivity * Time.deltaTime;

        xRotation -= mouseY;
        xRotation = Mathf.Clamp(xRotation, -89f, 89f);
        p_camera.transform.localRotation = Quaternion.Euler(xRotation, 0, 0);

        // Since we froze physics rotation, we can safely rotate the transform directly
        transform.Rotate(Vector3.up * mouseX);
    }

    private void HandleMovement()
    {
        Vector3 moveDir = transform.forward * _currentMoveInput.y + transform.right * _currentMoveInput.x;
        if (moveDir.magnitude &gt; 1) moveDir.Normalize();

        Vector3 targetVelocity = moveDir * maxSpeed;

        // Preserve gravity (Y velocity)
        Vector3 currentVelocity = new Vector3(rb.linearVelocity.x, 0, rb.linearVelocity.z);

        float accel = isGround ? groundAccel : airAccel;

        // Simple de-acceleration logic
        if (_currentMoveInput.magnitude &lt; 0.1f)
        {
            accel = deAccel;
            targetVelocity = Vector3.zero;
        }

        Vector3 newVelocity = Vector3.MoveTowards(currentVelocity, targetVelocity, accel * Time.fixedDeltaTime);

        rb.linearVelocity = new Vector3(newVelocity.x, rb.linearVelocity.y, newVelocity.z);
    }

    private void HandleJump()
    {
        if (isGround &amp;&amp; _jumpInputReceived)
        {
            // Reset Y velocity for consistent jump height
            rb.linearVelocity = new Vector3(rb.linearVelocity.x, 0, rb.linearVelocity.z);
            rb.AddForce(Vector3.up * jumpForce, ForceMode.Impulse);

            _jumpInputReceived = false;
        }
        // Optional: consume input if not grounded to prevent bunny hopping on landing
        else if (_jumpInputReceived &amp;&amp; !isGround)
        {
             _jumpInputReceived = false;
        }
    }

    private void OnTriggerStay(Collider other) =&gt; isGround = true;
    private void OnTriggerExit(Collider other) =&gt; isGround = false;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Triangulating matrices using LU decomposition method in C : Question about for loop optimization A: <p>To include the second nested loop in the first, you need to see where it changes a value that will be used in the sequence:</p>
<p>the calculation</p>
<pre><code>lu[i + j][i + k] = (lu[i + j][i + k] * lu[i - 1][i - 1] - lu[i - 1][i + k] * lu[i + j][i - 1]) / lu[i - 1][i - 1];
</code></pre>
<p>uses the line <code>i - 1</code>, so the <code>lu[i][j] = lu[i][j] / lu[j][j]</code> assignment in the second loop can only be made after the <code>lu[i+1]</code> assignment in the first loop.</p>
<p>In other words, in the <code>m</code> iteration of the first loop, you can only calculate <code>lu[m][j] = lu[m][j] / lu[j][j]</code> for <code>m = i - 1</code>.</p>
<p><code>lu[d-1]</code> need to be calculated apart, since it is not covered by the loop.</p>
<p>The second nested loop uses the condition <code>i &gt; j</code>, wich implies <code>i &gt; j &gt;= 0</code>, then <code>i &gt; 0</code>. So we could start the loop with <code>i = 1</code>.</p>
<p>Also, the same condition lead us to <code>d &gt; i &gt; j</code>, then (because <code>i</code> is integer), <code>d &gt; d - 1 &gt; j</code> or, in other words, <code>j &lt; d - 1</code>.</p>
<p>To do the inner-loop of the the second loop in the first, we need to add a extra loop from <code>j = d-i</code>.</p>
<pre><code>void tri (double lu[d][d])
{
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; d; i++)
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; d-i; j++)
        {
            for (int k = 0; k &lt; d-i; k++)
                lu[i+j][i+k] = (lu[i+j][i+k]*lu[i-1][i-1] - lu[i-1][i+k]*lu[i+j][i-1]) / lu[i-1][i-1];
            if (i - 1 &gt; j)
                lu[i-1][j] = lu[i-1][j] / lu[j][j];
        }
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; d-i; j++) //last line
            lu[d-1][j] = lu[d-1][j] / lu[j][j];
        for (int j = d-i; j &lt; d-1; j++)
            if (i &gt; j)
                lu[i][j] = lu[i-1][j] / lu[j][j];
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Using the min() CSS function causes ancestors to increase in width A: <p>max-width does not change the <em>intrinsic width</em> of an image.</p>
<p>Your image is naturally <strong>800px</strong>, so even though it is visually scaled down to 400px, its ancestors still size themselves as if the image were 800px wide.</p>
<p>max-width: min(400px, 100%) only limits how large the image <em>may become</em>, but it does <strong>not</strong> change the width used for shrink-to-fit calculations.</p>
<pre><code>img {
  width: min(400px, 100%);
}
</code></pre>
